Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a friendship occurs between two people of different status (father/son, elder/younger, ruler/subject, etc.), the affection, respect, and service exchanged between the two ought to be proportional to the status differential between the two.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important to understand friendships between two people with different statuses.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>In such a friendship, the one who benefitted financially or morally should give the other person honor in return, since that is the only way for the lesser to repay the greater.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a><ul><li>The other case.</li></ul></li></ul>","A post-Christian culture is not sustainable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Today's world is a post-Christian one, where the ideas originally from Christianity are taken out of their original religion context and adopted without such a basis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means (perhaps anticipates) the return to prominence of religion.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Romanticism was the first form of this, as it was when the human desire to communion with the infinite and real became detached from Christianity, instead attaching itself onto nature.<ul><li>However, due to the fact that the structure of nature is not metaphysically large enough to contain the whole of the idea of the sublime and the infinite, Romantic works of art thus showcase a melancholic and yearning side.</li></ul></li><li>Liberalism inherits the idea of universal human dignity and the primacy of conscience, maintaining both while not adopting the metaphysical foundations that underly such a belief. <ul><li>This is why cracks in liberal beliefs began to form in the 20th century and continues today.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Quote</p><p>Romanticism is not a temperamental preference shared by a group of hypersensitive poets, though it was indeed vulgarized into that. It is a Christian longing for the infinite surviving the collapse of the ground that gave it its proper object and its proper discipline, and finding nothing adequate to attach itself to.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Even attempts at overcoming are not successful, because ultimately, there is no overcome, but merely the performance of an overcoming. <ul><li>Nietzsche’s <em>Ubermensch</em> is, for instance, just a return to the prelapsarian innocence of man, a “Christian eschatological figure with the theology removed.”</li></ul></li><li>Since the end of WWII, the world has been running based on a “negative,” fueled by the narrative of “never again.” However, this structure is not able to remain stable forever, and has already fallen apart due to the weight of the metaphysical meaning attached to it.</li><li>We are now living through the recoil, when Christianity is coming back into fashion as a means of replenishing the depleting metaphysical reserves.<ul><li>New Atheism was the final attempt to halt this.</li></ul></li><li>The post-Christian world is associated with kitsch, where things become meaningless as become mere aesthetic objects, and this aestheticization is fetishized and itself aestheticized. <ul><li>This means that rather than actually being aware of the lack of meaning in art, with the aestheticization of aesthetics, a genuine reckoning with what is lacking disappears.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Philosophy of History</h4><p>When it comes to the philosophy of history, our modern conception of secular progress is also something that is unsustainable or at least metaphysically questionable without Christianity. As Lowith argues, when the Christian put their faith in God, it is not as much a debate as the relative reasonableness of such a belief, but that whether such faith can be put into the “God-Man” and not God himself.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 206</p><p><strong>Hope is justified only by faith which justifies itself.</strong> Perhaps both grow only on the ruins of all-too-human beliefs and expectations, on the fruitful soil of despair of what is subject to illusions and deceptions.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Examples of the cracks in liberal belief today.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>Perhaps a post-Christian world would end this too?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Similar claim to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A right is a claim to something which the society has an obligation to defend me in having that thing.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Mill’s definition of a right.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When a right a violated, the following occurs:</p><ul><li>Some individual is hurt.</li><li>There is a demand for the punishment of the offender.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible\">Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible</a><ul><li>This could be the definition of a right that Rawls would accept, and would thus clarify the principle stated here.</li></ul></li></ul>","A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is a possible society where the prospects of the worst off can be improved marginally, but the prospects of all those better are extremely negatively affected. This seems hardly acceptable, and yet, would be supported by Rawls’ views. Rawls argues that this is impossible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common and powerful objection against Rawls’ views.</p><h4>Counterargument</h4><p>Rawls argues that this is simply an inconceivable situation, since with realistic institutional assumptions, this situation could never occur. Specifically, his close-knit and chain-connected assumptions (see <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected\">The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected</a>) entail that improving the position of the least advantaged will in practice tend to improve or leave unchanged the positions of the better-off — making the extreme sensitivity scenario empirically implausible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a><ul><li>If this objection works, then this is no longer true.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>But under this edge case, it seems wrong to demand the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Play, games, joking, or other forms of recreation or rest is considered to be amusement, which is choice-worthy and pleasant, but it is not the happiness that one seeks.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This distinguishes amusements from happiness, which is an important distinction to make.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Amusements are not an end; rather they are a sort of means to provide relieve from exertion for ourselves so that we may work.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The end goal of life is not amusement, and it is not something that one should consider an end at all.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>Rather, the contemplative life is true happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Ceteris paribus, an ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic. This would maximize their revenue, ignoring any costs that act as a function of quantity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as this explains why the ordinary monopolist set the price where the demand curve is unit elastic, which explains why there is often a shortage and higher prices in markets a monopoly.</p><h4>Proof</h4><p>Let the revenue function for some monopolist be defined as such: $R(p)=p \\cdot q(p)$, where $p$ represents the price and $q(p)$ represents the quantity supplied as a function of price. To find the price at which the monopolist maximizes profits, then, let us find where $R'(p)=0$. $$ R'(p)=pq'(p) \\cdot q(p) $$ $$ 0 = 1 + \\frac{p}{q(p)}q'(p) $$ The the point-slope definition of elasticity ($\\epsilon$) is $\\frac{p}{q} \\cdot \\frac{dq}{dp}$. Thus, where monopolistic revenue maximization occurs is at $$\\epsilon = -1$$</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is one of the main theses in Friedman’s <em>Capitalism and Freedom</em>, with him arguing that it is economic freedom and underlies political freedom. Without economic freedom, political freedom cannot stand.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a strong argument for promoting economic freedom, since political freedom is the cornerstone of so many democracies worldwide.</p><h4>References</h4>","Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that seems to be uniquely humans is the ability to preserve information and be able to pass in through time to our descendants. In this way, we preserve information for our descendants and thus cooperate with them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is unique to humanity, and it gives us an edge when it comes to combating existential risks.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since we are able to interact with the future (at least in some non-trivial sense), it means that we are far more effective at dealing with problems that concern long terms than other species. However, this characteristic has its limits too, and it seems that currently, humanity does not think long-term <em>enough</em> in order to deal with the existential risks that abound. Another consequence of this is the existence of the study of population ethics, or how we ought to treat people yet to exist.</p><h4>References</h4>","NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The July 1995 massacre of Bosnians by the Serb army was the worst massacre post-WWII, and led to the involvement of NATO forces in Bosnia in order to prevent further war crimes from being committed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the immediate spark for NATO intervention in the Bosnian War.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete\">The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete</a><ul><li>A competing account of the same intervention. That note reads it as civilizational betrayal driven by naivete, not as a response to atrocity.</li></ul></li></ul>","Religion is compatible with science":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that religion/faith is able to be reconciled with science, despite the typically dogmatic approach that faith holds towards belief.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a project that has been attempted by many.</p><h4><em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></h4><p>This is something that Dostoevsky attempts to accomplish in <em>TBK</em>. He attempts to existentialize religion to make it both not coerce belief (the existence of miracles, in his view, would coerce belief), but ensure that the individual can still believe. He does this through the extraction of the existential core of Christian rituals. Some examples of the rituals that he is able to existentialize include:</p><ul><li><strong>Confession</strong>: public/communal confession of sin to all of the world, since one is responsible for everything.<ul><li>The moral “butterfly effect.”</li></ul></li><li><strong>The incarnation</strong>: someone receives a divine spirit when they embrace agape and the spirit of loving all that is around them.</li><li><strong>Ordination of priests</strong>: God enters first Markel, then Zossima, then Alyosha.</li><li><strong>The crucifixion</strong>: when people experience suffering, they can<ul><li>“lacerate” themselves, they “crucify” themselves to feel a sense of moral superiority to others. But is equivalent to being crucified, and staying that way.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Immortality</strong>: ?</li><li><strong>Angels</strong>: someone who is able to, with agape, bring out the good in others.<ul><li>Ex. Alyosha to Grushenka.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Devils</strong>: someone who desires to bring out the worst in others.<ul><li>Ex. Rakitin when he tried to make Alyosha sin.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Miracles</strong>: when something unexpected situation is created through love.<ul><li>Ex. When Alyosha saves Grushenka.</li></ul></li><li>After Zossima dies, the book gives specific, scientific reason for why the body stinks. This ruined the miracle of the death of Zossima.<ul><li>Tangentially, this also leads to a lot of disbelief, as people prove right the thesis of the GI, that they are weak and need guidance and miracles for faith.</li></ul></li><li>But eventually Alyosha is able to overcome this through a realization of his own, something that comes internally.</li></ul><p>Religion is not observed through miracles that break the laws of physics; rather, it is observed through human interconnection.</p><h4>References</h4>","Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The purpose of having a historical consciousness is to be able to establish an active continuity with the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was an idea espoused by Burckhardt.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, the continuity mentioned here is not a merely passive sort of continuity, or as Burckhardt says, “accepting the cake of custom.” Rather, it means consciously remembering and renewing one’s heritage. Burckhardt argues that this is the only way in which historical consciousness is meaningful, since there is not a grand, overarching, teleology to history, nor is history deterministic in the way that the ancient Greeks thought.</p><ul><li>Although Burckhardt did believe that human nature did not change, in the same way that the Greek did.</li></ul><p>Rather, Burckhardt argues that the reason for the active preservation of our historical past is that such is the only proof of the “significance of the duration of our existence.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a><ul><li>Related.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Perhaps the idea of continuity is related to the idea of traditionalism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Strong induction, which can only occur in a conductor, sees charge moving through the entirety of the object, such that entire areas of the object can shift in charge, creating a polarization effect on the scale of the entire object. On the other hand, local polarization, which can only occur in an insulator,</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>The conductor/insulator split is exactly what determines which effect occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor\">Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule\">The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</a></li></ul>","The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Buddhism was overtaken by the <em>Bhakti</em> movement, a movement with roots in Hinduism, around the turn of the first milenium.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Spread through Indian between 600 - 1300 A.D., and overtook Buddhism.</li><li>Made Hindu practice more accessible to the common people, even people of low caste, since it focused on intense worship of deities, rather than philosophical speculation or sacrifices, often conducted by the Brahmins.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there\">Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there</a></li></ul>","The Future of Freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This book talks about democracy, freedom, and constitutional liberalism, and related concepts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This book presents an alternative view for that is common thought of in our view of democracy and freedom.</p><h4>Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Stranger":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Meursault, a French Algerian clerk, learns that his mother has died and attends her funeral without showing much emotion. He starts a relationship with Marie, befriends his neighbor Raymond, and becomes involved in Raymond’s conflict with some Arabs. One day at the beach, Meursault later goes back alone, meets one of the Arabs, and kills him with a gun after being overwhelmed by the heat and sunlight. He is arrested, put on trial, and sentenced to death, with the court focusing as much on his behavior and lack of feeling as on the murder itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the most important novels which explores the philosophical position of Absurdism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome\">Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value\">The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value</a></li></ul><h4>Philosophy</h4><p>In a sense, what this book is trying to say is that if the conventional view of the world is as being in two dimensions of “value” and “aesthetics,” the most sensible way to approach the world is to collapse the dimension of “value.” The apparent meaninglessness of the universe provides no evidence of the objectiveness of values and thus, gives us no reason to maintain a belief in such a socially constructed facade. Although doing so would mean isolating oneself from the community, who continue to hold such values, such isolation ought to be of no concern. Although the community will condemn the person who is Absurd that their philosophy is fundamentally destabilizing and corrupting, it seems to capture the truth in a more accurate manner. To care about the condemn nation of others is to concede that there is at least one value which exists. In realizing that other people are likewise simply corpses waiting to be buried is comfort. The understanding of the fact that the universe itself is meaningless already provides a sense of fraternity. To hold no values and yet enjoy aesthetics is a life that is possible to live, and indeed, it seems rational to live as such.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible\">Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible</a><ul><li>A characteristic of Camus’ writing, contrasted with Kafka.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arguably, the main reason for the collapse of the Han was the weak central state and the increasing power of warlords across the nation. Due to the central government’s inability to quell the Yellow Turbans, they had to call for help from regional warlords, greatly strengthening their power, eventually allowing then to challenge imperial power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a main reason for the collapse of the Han dynasty, which threw China into the Three Kingdoms Period.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li></ul>","The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The basic unit of many nomadic groups was the tribe. This meant that power almost always fell to the strongest, since the tribe’s survival was dependent on the leadership of the leader, and weak leaders would be abandoned.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the context in which mongol society developed, which speaks to the way that they approach life and warfare.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a><ul><li>What Genghis Khan had to overcome: the tribe was the default unit, and unification meant superseding it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere\">Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere</a><ul><li>The environment that made the tribe, rather than the settlement, the viable unit.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are a few reasons why the difference principle is preferred by Rawls over utilitarian principles:</p><ul><li>Utilitarian principles require that we make comparisons of cardinal values (utilities) across individuals, which is difficult to do.</li><li>Utilitarian principles compare subjective “utilities,” while the difference principle compares more objective and observable primary goods.<ul><li>People may have different utility functions.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Rawls uses the difference principle over something like utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For these reasons, Rawls thinks that it is better to embed the moral ideas directly into the principles, such as through the difference principle. Additionally, it seems difficult if not impossible to do an accurate utility calculation from behind the veil of ignorance, since you don’t know the utility function of the actual people in the society.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Another means of satisfying such needs is through a more utilitarian framework, which is defeated here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The division of labor degrades the mind, which independently justifies universal public education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Specialisation confines workers to repetitive tasks that erode the very capacities that made them capable, so a state may owe them education as a remedy for what economic organisation does to them, quite apart from any gain in output.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The argument grounds public education in a harm caused by the market rather than in the returns to human capital, which makes it available to someone who rejects the usual economic case for schooling.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith argues in this form, which is notable given how often he is enlisted against public provision. His economic policy has social and moral objectives and not merely the maximisation of national income.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>Accepts the obligation and disputes the monopoly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a><ul><li>The stronger position this argument is sometimes taken to support.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>The same conclusion from a virtue-based rather than a remedial premise.</li></ul></li></ul>","The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits in the economy is one that is inherently utilitarian, as any deontological system would prohibit acting in an self-interested way. Only a utilitarian conception of the world would allow for self-interest to take place in an economic system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This crucial because it seems to reveal an ideological inconsistency in the right. Typically, right wing thinkers are the most supportive of capitalist theory, and yet often believe in structured moral theory. This seems to be a contradiction.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li></ul>","The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The third wave of democracy began around 1974 and ended around 1991.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This defines the end of the Cold War and saw many of the formerly Communist nations democratize.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The third wave began with the Carnation Revolution in Portugal, and spread to many areas around the world, including the Four Asian Tigers and Eastern Europe following the Collapse of the Soviet Union.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li></ul>","Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Uncertainty in the economy leads to slowed economic growth, in terms of diminished consumption, hiring, and investment. It also weakens the effect of monetary and fiscal policy. However, its effects in the long run are indeterminate, as uncertainty may in fact encourage R&amp;D and innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Today, uncertainty, especially under the Trump administration is especially prominent. It is important to understand the significance of such uncertainty, especially for the economy in the short and long term.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Uncertainty in countercyclical, meaning that it rises during period of economic downturn. This also means that it can worsen recessions, as it can lead to poorer quality of information, heightened policy experimentation, and generally amplify volatility. However, it has ambiguous long term consequences since it seems to have beneficial effects on innovation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a><ul><li>Also regarding the effect of uncertainty on the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even though the original position is a hypothetical decision scenario, it still makes sense for us to believe in its methodological power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls justifies the use of the original position.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although it is hypothetical, it is made up of a collection of axioms which we would independently accept (this is by definition, since our own description of the original position utilizes our own axioms; in this way, the heavily epistemic work is done when Rawls attempts to convince us of the truth of his particular set of axioms).</p><ul><li>These principles outline the limits of what we would accept as fair terms of social cooperation.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","We should never halt technological innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should never actively halt technological innovation, since that would in fact be an existential catastrophe, since it entails a destruction of humanity’s future potential.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a unique take on the argument that humanity should never halt technological development, despite any temporary downsides that it may have.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise\">Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise</a><ul><li>This is a potential problem with this claim, since new technologies can be abused or misused to hurt humans.</li></ul></li></ul>","When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When two conductors touch, their combined charge remains the same. However, the charges are individually evenly distributed across the two conductors.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the law of charge conservation in action, and is relevant for AP Physics C E&amp;M.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Given two conductors, one with $Q$ and the other with $2Q$ charge, when the two conductors touch, they both end up with $1.5Q$.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li>Charges always refers to either electrons or ions<ul><li>Relevant for understanding the content of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of conductors, which is also relevant for understanding this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While the state has a right to require that parents give their children education, the state cannot be the only provider of education.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This composes a part of Mill’s thoughts on education.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The state must require education since Mill claims that life would be exceedingly dull without basic education. The state, however, cannot be the sole provider of education, since then everybody would be educated in the same way, and we want people to be able to be educated in different ways to produce different types of people. The state ca provide education in cases where the parent’s can’t afford it otherwise, etc.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should do as little as possible\">The state should do as little as possible</a><ul><li>The Taoists would not agree with this.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a><ul><li>Aristotle would argue that the state should entirely be in charge of education, for the cultivation of virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Wild Strawberries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Professor Isak Borg, a cold, egotistical 78-year-old, drives to Lund for an honorary doctorate with his estranged daughter-in-law Marianne. Along the way, hitchhikers and a bitter couple stir up memories of the sweetheart who left him for his brother and his own unhappy marriage, while nightmares force him to face his guilt and coldness. Slowly accepting his life and nearing death, he receives the degree and drifts off dreaming peacefully of a family picnic by a lake.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Perhaps Bergman’s best work, certainly an insightful reflection on death.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Life is too short for regrets\">Life is too short for regrets</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, one has nothing to regret\">In life, one has nothing to regret</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Yi Yi\">Yi Yi</a><ul><li>Explores similar themes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"East of Eden\">East of Eden</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
